# Valgrind suppressions for `rake spec:valgrind` (ruby_memcheck).
#
# ruby_memcheck auto-loads every `<suppressions>/<ruby-version>.supp` it finds,
# and `ruby.supp` (the bare RUBY_ENGINE name) matches every Ruby version. These
# entries are ADDED to the ones the gem already bundles.
#
# The job checks invalid reads/writes and "definitely lost" leaks; it runs with
# `--undef-value-errors=no` (rakelib/valgrind.rake says why), so there is no
# uninitialised-value entry here. What is left are leaks the VM, or a cache of
# ours that lives until exit by design, never frees. We do NOT suppress
# Addr/Overlap/Param/etc.

# ---- Ruby VM internal caches that are never freed before exit ----
# The global constant-cache / inline method-cache id_table is allocated lazily
# on method lookup and lives for the life of the VM, so RUBY_FREE_AT_EXIT still
# reports it as "definitely lost" whenever the first lookup happened under one
# of our cfuncs. Not ours to free.
{
  ruby-vm-method-cache-id_table
  Memcheck:Leak
  ...
  fun:rb_id_table_create
  fun:vm_search_cc
  ...
}

# The global symbol table (`global_symbols.ids`) grows in chunks as symbols are
# interned, and lives for the life of the VM: RUBY_FREE_AT_EXIT reports its
# chunk arrays as "definitely lost" whenever the growth happened under one of
# our cfuncs - `parse_stylesheet` interns its hash keys with `rb_intern` on the
# first call, and whether that call crosses a chunk boundary depends on how many
# symbols the process made before it. Not ours to free.
{
  ruby-global-symbol-table
  Memcheck:Leak
  ...
  fun:set_id_entry*
  ...
}

# ---- Makiri's process-lifetime CSS compiled-selector cache ----
# `css_cache` keeps compiled selector lists in a process-global
# `HashMap<Box<[u8]>, _>` (bounded by CACHE_CAP) that lives until exit by
# design. hashbrown holds only a pointer INTO its allocation (at the control
# bytes), never to its start, so the exit-time leak scan calls the table and
# every key inside it "possibly lost". Both are allocated under
# `Cache::compile` (`with_compiled` is inlined into the glue, so it is not in
# the stack; the old engine's `with_compiled_selector` this entry used to name
# is test-only now). `possible` only: a real `definite` leak on this path still
# fails the run.
{
  makiri-css-selector-cache
  Memcheck:Leak
  match-leak-kinds: possible
  ...
  fun:*css_cache*Cache*compile*
}
